Task 1: Configure Multiple IdM Replicas for Redundancy
Subtask 1.1: Verify Primary IdM Server Status
First, let's ensure our primary IdM server is running properly and check its current status.

Connect to the primary IdM server:
ssh root@idm-master
Check IdM server status:
ipactl status
Verify IdM server configuration:
ipa config-show
Check current topology:
ipa topologysegment-find domain
ipa topologysegment-find ca
Verify DNS configuration:
dig SRV _ldap._tcp.example.com
dig SRV _kerberos._tcp.example.com
Subtask 1.2: Prepare Replica Servers
Before creating replicas, we need to prepare the target servers with proper hostnames, DNS, and network configuration.

Connect to the first replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica1
Set proper hostname:
hostnamectl set-hostname idm-replica1.example.com
Update /etc/hosts file:
cat >> /etc/hosts << EOF
192.168.1.10 idm-master.example.com idm-master
192.168.1.11 idm-replica1.example.com idm-replica1
192.168.1.12 idm-replica2.example.com idm-replica2
EOF
Configure DNS to point to IdM master:
echo "nameserver 192.168.1.10" > /etc/resolv.conf
echo "search example.com" >> /etc/resolv.conf
Install required packages:
dnf install -y ipa-server ipa-server-dns
Repeat steps 1-5 for the second replica server (idm-replica2).
Subtask 1.3: Create the First IdM Replica
Now we'll create our first replica server to provide redundancy for our IdM infrastructure.

Return to the primary IdM server:
ssh root@idm-master
Authenticate as admin:
kinit admin
Create replica preparation file:
ipa-replica-prepare idm-replica1.example.com --ip-address=192.168.1.11
Copy the replica file to the target server:
scp /var/lib/ipa/replica-info-idm-replica1.example.com.gpg root@idm-replica1:/tmp/
Connect to the first replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica1
Install the replica:
ipa-replica-install /tmp/replica-info-idm-replica1.example.com.gpg \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4
Start and enable IdM services:
ipactl start
systemctl enable ipa
Verify replica installation:
ipactl status
ipa-replica-conncheck --master idm-master.example.com
Subtask 1.4: Create the Second IdM Replica
Let's add a second replica for additional redundancy and load distribution.

Return to the primary IdM server:
ssh root@idm-master
Create second replica preparation file:
ipa-replica-prepare idm-replica2.example.com --ip-address=192.168.1.12
Copy the replica file to the second target server:
scp /var/lib/ipa/replica-info-idm-replica2.example.com.gpg root@idm-replica2:/tmp/
Connect to the second replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica2
Install the second replica:
ipa-replica-install /tmp/replica-info-idm-replica2.example.com.gpg \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4
Start and enable IdM services:
ipactl start
systemctl enable ipa
Subtask 1.5: Verify Replication Topology
After creating both replicas, let's verify that our replication topology is properly configured.

From any IdM server, check the topology:
kinit admin
ipa topologysegment-find domain
Check CA replication topology:
ipa topologysegment-find ca
Verify all servers are reachable:
ipa server-find
Check replication agreements:
ipa-replica-manage list
Task 2: Test Replication and Failover
Subtask 2.1: Test Data Replication
Let's create test data and verify it replicates across all IdM servers.

Connect to the primary IdM server:
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
Create a test user:
ipa user-add testuser1 \
  --first=Test \
  --last=User1 \
  --email=testuser1@example.com \
  --password
Create a test group:
ipa group-add testgroup1 --desc="Test Group 1"
Add user to group:
ipa group-add-member testgroup1 --users=testuser1
Verify data on first replica:
ssh root@idm-replica1
kinit admin
ipa user-show testuser1
ipa group-show testgroup1
Verify data on second replica:
ssh root@idm-replica2
kinit admin
ipa user-show testuser1
ipa group-show testgroup1
Subtask 2.2: Test Bidirectional Replication
Now let's test that changes made on replicas also propagate to other servers.

Connect to first replica:
ssh root@idm-replica1
kinit admin
Create a user from replica1:
ipa user-add testuser2 \
  --first=Test \
  --last=User2 \
  --email=testuser2@example.com \
  --password
Connect to second replica:
ssh root@idm-replica2
kinit admin
Create a user from replica2:
ipa user-add testuser3 \
  --first=Test \
  --last=User3 \
  --email=testuser3@example.com \
  --password
Verify all users exist on master:
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
ipa user-find --all
Check replication status:
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Subtask 2.3: Test Failover Scenarios
Let's simulate server failures and test how clients handle failover.

Configure a test client:
ssh root@client1
Install IdM client:
dnf install -y ipa-client
Join client to IdM domain:
ipa-client-install \
  --domain=example.com \
  --server=idm-master.example.com \
  --server=idm-replica1.example.com \
  --server=idm-replica2.example.com \
  --unattended
Test authentication before failover:
kinit testuser1
klist
Simulate master server failure:
ssh root@idm-master
ipactl stop
Test authentication during failover:
ssh root@client1
kinit testuser2
klist
Verify client can still access IdM services:
ipa user-show testuser1
Restart master server:
ssh root@idm-master
ipactl start
Subtask 2.4: Monitor Replication Performance
Let's check replication performance and identify any potential issues.

Check replication lag:
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Monitor LDAP replication:
ldapsearch -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  -b "cn=replica,cn=dc\=example\,dc\=com,cn=mapping tree,cn=config" \
  -s sub "(objectclass=*)"
Check replication status on all servers:
for server in idm-master idm-replica1 idm-replica2; do
  echo "=== $server ==="
  ssh root@$server "ipa-replica-manage list"
done
Task 3: Troubleshoot Replication Issues Using ipa-replica-manage
Subtask 3.1: Identify Common Replication Problems
Let's learn how to identify and diagnose common replication issues.

Check for broken replication agreements:
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
ipa-replica-manage list --verbose
Check replication status:
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Examine replication conflicts:
ldapsearch -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  -b "dc=example,dc=com" \
  "(nsds5ReplConflict=*)"
Check server connectivity:
ipa-replica-conncheck --master idm-replica1.example.com
ipa-replica-conncheck --master idm-replica2.example.com
Subtask 3.2: Simulate and Fix Replication Issues
Let's create some replication issues and learn how to resolve them.

Create a replication conflict scenario:
# On master
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
ipa user-add conflictuser --first=Conflict --last=Master --password

# Simultaneously on replica1 (simulate network partition)
ssh root@idm-replica1
iptables -A INPUT -s 192.168.1.10 -j DROP
ipa user-add conflictuser --first=Conflict --last=Replica --password
Restore connectivity:
ssh root@idm-replica1
iptables -D INPUT -s 192.168.1.10 -j DROP
Check for conflicts:
ldapsearch -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  -b "dc=example,dc=com" \
  "(nsds5ReplConflict=*)"
Resolve conflicts manually:
# Identify the conflict entry
ldapsearch -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  -b "uid=conflictuser,cn=users,cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com" \
  "(objectclass=*)"

# Delete the conflict entry
ldapdelete -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  "uid=conflictuser + nsuniqueid=...,cn=users,cn=accounts,dc=example,dc=com"
Subtask 3.3: Reinitialize Broken Replicas
Sometimes replicas become severely out of sync and need to be reinitialized.

Check replication status:
ssh root@idm-master
ipa-replica-manage list --verbose
Force reinitialize a replica:
ipa-replica-manage re-initialize --from idm-master.example.com idm-replica1.example.com
Monitor reinitialize progress:
tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-EXAMPLE-COM/access
Verify reinitialize completion:
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Subtask 3.4: Remove and Re-add Problematic Replicas
In extreme cases, you may need to completely remove and re-add a replica.

Remove a replica from topology:
ssh root@idm-master
kinit admin
ipa server-del idm-replica2.example.com --force
Clean up the removed server:
ssh root@idm-replica2
ipa-server-install --uninstall
Re-add the replica:
# Prepare new replica file
ssh root@idm-master
ipa-replica-prepare idm-replica2.example.com --ip-address=192.168.1.12

# Copy and install
scp /var/lib/ipa/replica-info-idm-replica2.example.com.gpg root@idm-replica2:/tmp/
ssh root@idm-replica2
ipa-replica-install /tmp/replica-info-idm-replica2.example.com.gpg \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8
Subtask 3.5: Advanced Troubleshooting Techniques
Let's explore advanced troubleshooting methods for complex replication issues.

Enable detailed replication logging:
ssh root@idm-master
ldapmodify -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W << EOF
dn: cn=config
changetype: modify
replace: nsslapd-errorlog-level
nsslapd-errorlog-level: 8192
EOF
Monitor replication in real-time:
tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-EXAMPLE-COM/errors | grep -i repl
Check replication agreement details:
ldapsearch -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W \
  -b "cn=config" \
  "(objectclass=nsds5replicationagreement)"
Manually trigger replication:
ldapmodify -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W << EOF
dn: cn=meTo_idm-replica1.example.com,cn=replica,cn=dc\=example\,dc\=com,cn=mapping tree,cn=config
changetype: modify
replace: nsds5BeginReplicaRefresh
nsds5BeginReplicaRefresh: start
EOF
Reset replication logging:
ldapmodify -x -D "cn=Directory Manager" -W << EOF
dn: cn=config
changetype: modify
replace: nsslapd-errorlog-level
nsslapd-errorlog-level: 266354688
EOF
